WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:34.430 --> 00:00:35.430
On Friday.

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:39.160
I'm going to talk about Joe Biden's recent
campaign strategy, if you can even call it

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.700
that, of sort of laying low and not being
super public.

00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:47.630
But today I want to update you about polling
since it's been about a almost two weeks since

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:54.080
I last did and it's actually more bad news
for Joe Biden, Joe Biden's polls keep dropping.

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:59.800
We're going to put the trajectory up on the
screen for our video audience, and the story

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:06.869
has been that before announcing his candidacy,
Joe Biden was pulling around the mid twenties

00:01:06.869 --> 00:01:11.260
up to the high twenties and then eventually
Joe Biden announced and said, I'm actually

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.420
running for president, and that spiked up
to higher than 40 support for Joe Biden.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:20.130
But since then it hasn't been so good and
there's been a regular trailing off of Joe

00:01:20.130 --> 00:01:21.130
Biden.

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:25.560
Last time I talked about this, Joe Biden's
polling had trailed down to about 35 or 36%

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:32.930
and the last week has seen more drops to an
average of between 33 and 34% for Joe Biden.

00:01:32.930 --> 00:01:38.241
Now a quick note here before we continue the
analysis, if you never believed that Joe Biden

00:01:38.241 --> 00:01:42.280
was polling as high as he was a month ago
because the Poles were supposedly fake, which

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:46.200
I don't believe, right, but if you believe
that, then you should also not believe that

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.250
Joe Biden's polling has been trailing off.

00:01:48.250 --> 00:01:52.220
It's sort of weird to see the same people
that say the polling is totally fake.

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:56.490
Now site the polling as evidenced that Joe
Biden is losing support.

00:01:56.490 --> 00:01:57.979
Uh, it's one or the other.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:01:59.270
You can't have it both ways.

00:01:59.270 --> 00:02:03.479
It's like Trump saying the employment numbers
are fake under Obama, but they're real under

00:02:03.479 --> 00:02:07.190
him and an indicator that the economy is doing
really well doesn't work.

00:02:07.190 --> 00:02:08.640
Okay, so don't do that.

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:13.580
But in any case who has been gaining from
Joe Biden's polling drops up.

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:17.730
Bernie Sanders has seen a slight uptick into
the high teens.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:23.540
Elizabeth Warren has seen a very small uptick,
but most recently it's actually been Pete

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:29.420
booted judge who has been climbing in the
polls and very slightly Kamala Harris, a lot

00:02:29.420 --> 00:02:31.610
of people are still just going nowhere.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:37.370
I mean Tulsi Gabbert continuing to pull about
half of 1% unable to get it to move.

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:42.550
Jay Inslee, Tim Ryan, John Delaney, Bullock,
Gillette brand, Williamson, they can't even

00:02:42.550 --> 00:02:43.950
get to 1%.

00:02:43.950 --> 00:02:50.370
Um, but there have been two sort of major
opposing views on the Biden drop and what's

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:52.660
causing Biden's decline.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:58.489
One view is Biden staying out of the public
eye is hurting him because people are forgetting

00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:01.180
about them and people aren't supporting him.

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.360
Because there just aren't aware of his presence.

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:09.500
The other view is that since Joe Biden publicly
announced more people are understanding his

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:11.870
platform and they don't like it.

00:03:11.870 --> 00:03:14.990
And thus people who thought, oh, well I kind
of liked the idea of Biden.

00:03:14.990 --> 00:03:20.060
It's sort of reminiscent of the Obama era
as they learn about Joe Biden's platform.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:26.000
They are moving away from Joe Biden either
or both could be true or neither depending

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:29.660
on who you're talking about, which part of
the country you're talking about.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:36.690
But in the immediate, the question becomes
the debates and Iowa being the first primary

00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:37.690
or caucus.

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:45.920
Uh, the reality is that Iowa depends on relatively
few voters and Biden is still doing well in

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:50.830
Iowa, although he is trailing off there in
the same way that he has been nationally.

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:55.791
Here's my advice to you, if you don't like
the way this primary is shaking up, uh, in

00:03:55.791 --> 00:03:58.620
terms of the people that are getting more
attention, in terms of the people that are

00:03:58.620 --> 00:04:01.930
leading the polls, to the extent that you
believe the polls and generally I think the

00:04:01.930 --> 00:04:05.560
polls are a pretty good picture of what's
going on.

00:04:05.560 --> 00:04:09.990
Don't get mad at the Poles and the fact that
a candidate you don't like might be leading,

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:16.859
okay campaign for and be an activist for whoever
you think is the best candidate period.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:21.500
And soon the debates, we'll start and we'll
start to get a sense of whether the numbers

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.700
move, we'll start to get a sense of how people
who are going to be voting in the Democratic

00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:32.330
primary are actually reacting to these candidates
in real time, particularly not just to their

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.900
ideas but to how they relate to other candidates
that are on the stage.

00:04:35.900 --> 00:04:41.669
The reality is that historically in general,
in June of the year before an election, which

00:04:41.669 --> 00:04:46.930
is where we are now, this is like June of
2015 relative to the 2016 election.

00:04:46.930 --> 00:04:53.770
This is like June of 2011 relative to the
2012 election, June of 2019 in the context

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:55.450
of the 2020 election.

00:04:55.450 --> 00:05:02.139
The polls are pretty good at telling us who
is likely to win the nomination.

00:05:02.139 --> 00:05:03.139
Historically.

00:05:03.139 --> 00:05:06.819
We'll see if something is different this time
around.

00:05:06.819 --> 00:05:11.050
Uh, but that is a reality that we have to
contend with and we have to accept at this

00:05:11.050 --> 00:05:12.789
point in time.

00:05:12.789 --> 00:05:18.389
Generally speaking, the winner of the primary
is going to be someone who is already polling

00:05:18.389 --> 00:05:19.389
relatively well.

00:05:19.389 --> 00:05:24.321
It doesn't have to be the number one person,
but it's extraordinarily unlikely that it

00:05:24.321 --> 00:05:27.729
is going to be someone who's pulling below
two or 3% at this point in time.

00:05:27.729 --> 00:05:28.930
If you disagree, let me know.

